WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.800
These next five videos I'm going to
be covering everything Vitamin B12.

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.440
How fast B12 deficiency can set in,
the earliest signs to watch out for,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.680
why standard blood tests can miss it,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:15.520
and just how dangerous
it can be left untreated.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.514
Most importantly,
the best way to protect yourself.

00:00:18.514 --> 00:00:25.716
"How Long Does It Take to Become Vitamin B12 Deficient
and What Are the Symptoms?"

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.800
For those eating
plant-based diets,

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.280
encouragement
of vitamin B12 supplementation

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.920
cannot be overemphasized.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:37.280
What happens
if you don’t get enough B12?

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.360
Well, the vitamin is necessary
for the synthesis of DNA,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:45.120
so, a deficiency in B12
can cause pretty much anything.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.120
Brain problems,
psychiatric problems,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.120
sensory problems,
constitutional problems,

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.120
muscle problems,
nerve problems.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.120
It can also cause
clotting disorders

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.200
and bleeding disorders.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.360
It can make people catatonic,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:01.360
hallucinate, psychotic,
even suicidal.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.760
One patient
got electroshock therapy

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:04.560
before they realized,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.400
"Oops, he was just
B12 deficient."

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.280
Even more tragic are
life-threatening manifestations

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.920
of B12 deficiency in infants

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.120
when their moms
aren’t supplementing;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.040
coma, respiratory failure,
severe brain damage.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.360
Vitamin B12 deficiency
in pregnancy

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.960
is a diagnosis not to miss.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.600
I’ve done B12 videos like this

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.240
over and over again
over the years.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.640
Can we just stick
to the consensus

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:36.560
clinical guidelines
on preventing,

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.680
diagnosing,
and treating B12 deficiency?

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:43.440
No, we can’t
because there aren’t any.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.360
There are lots
of recommendations out there

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.000
but little robust evidence
to back them up.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.440
They’re based
more on assumptions

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:51.920
than solid clinical evidence.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.240
That’s why you’ll see
papers like this,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.680
"How I Treat
Vitamin B12 Deficiency."

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.120
Why should we care what
some random doctor does?

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.680
Because there aren’t
a lot of clear-cut answers

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.520
everyone agrees upon.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:07.920
For example,
it is currently unknown

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.720
how long it takes
for vitamin B12 deficiency

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.040
to occur in someone
going completely plant-based

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.440
without supplementing with B12
or B12-fortified foods.

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:19.360
This textbook
suggested it might take

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.520
as long as 10 or 20 years,

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.080
but that was based
on a study of baboons

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.840
and a single case report.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.680
On the flipside
are those suggesting

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.480
we can see evidence
of a functional B12 deficiency

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.160
within four weeks,

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.800
but if you look at that source,
that’s not right either.

00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:39.680
Three years is a common
time frame we hear,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.520
but that’s just from this
70-year-old autopsy study

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:46.080
where a back-of-the-envelope
calculation said

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.800
that if the average liver weighs
like three pounds (1.35 kg),

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.480
and B12 is stored in the liver,

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.480
and there’s a certain amount
of B12 per gram,

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.320
then the average liver
would contain enough

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:57.520
for three years,
but that’s just all in theory.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:58.720
If we look at people

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:00.960
undergoing a type
of bariatric surgery

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.520
that messes with B12 absorption,

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.240
we start seeing problems
within just a few months

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.400
in terms of impaired metabolism,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.880
a rise in methylmalonic acid
and homocysteine,

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.480
two compounds that are normally
cleared, in part, by B12.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.440
This challenges the concept
that liver stores of B12

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.280
were sufficient
for long-term maintenance

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.360
of adequate B12 status.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:23.040
Here’s an interesting
recent case.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.280
A vegan who had been taking B12

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:28.720
decided to stop it
for undisclosed reasons.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.120
This allowed
for a natural experiment.

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.400
Though his blood levels of B12
stayed within normal limits,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:37.600
his homocysteine
went through the roof.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.200
We like to see it
below 12 µmol/L,

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.080
but it jumped to 18 µmol/L
in a matter of months,

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.880
though there is
a complicating factor.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.600
His folate plunged
at the same time,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.120
and folate is also necessary
to detoxify homocysteine.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.520
Evidently, when he stopped
supplementing with B12,

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.240
he also stopped eating
as many fruits and vegetables,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.080
folate
is concentrated in greens,

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:00.800
because it was wintertime
and his access was limited,

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.800
so that may have
contributed, too.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:05.040
The bottom line,
the researchers concluded,

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:06.640
is that this
reiterates the need

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.640
for continuous
B12 supplementation

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.240
in persons following
an unfortified plant-based diet.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.160
Some medical professionals
even suggest

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.760
it might be
a good preventative measure

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:19.840
for all vegans to annually
check their B12 status.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.240
The number one question
primary care doctors

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.960
are advised to ask
symptomatic patients

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.800
on a plant-based diet is,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:31.412
“What Is Your Preferred
Source of Vitamin B12?”

